    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think she should just take the, you know, Obama, Bill Clinton approach of like, we've got to be tough on the border. You have to know who's in the country. There's no way around that. And we are a nation of laws. We promote, you know, global laws to try to keep ourselves organized, and you can't have people jump in the line. It's just not fair. It's not fair to those people who are doing it the right way. And that doesn't mean you can't accept people on asylum and people in really tragic situations in the country. We're big enough and big-hearted enough to absorb some of that. But I think she needs to embrace that Republican Democratic compromise bill and talk about what was in that, more border patrol, all of the investments, creating a process. Was it perfect? No. But it was led by guys that are sincere and good people and good conservatives like Senator Langford down in Oklahoma. And Trump did make calls to end that to use that as a political tool moving into the fall. And so you have to say that because I think it speaks to Trump, but embrace what was in that and say that. And then also, I think it's important to recognize that most of the fentanyl coming here comes in through the mail. I mean, you're talking about really, and this is where I, you know, while I like talking to guys like you, you're gonna have an actual conversation. Like it can't just be Demagog, one side or the other. What's the reality? Most of it's coming in through the United States Postal Service or UPS or whatever into the country. It's very, very small amounts that can turn into a lot of money for these drug dealers, which is why they're using it. You don't need like, you know, crate loads of cocaine. You can just literally ship a UPS or whatever to somebody here in the United States and they get it and they can make millions of dollars off it. And so it's very, very difficult. It's a national problem. And then the last thing I would highlight is overdose deaths 